<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><style>@page{size:A4;margin:1in 1in 1in 1.5in;}body{font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;line-height:1.5;}h1{font-size:17pt;text-transform:uppercase;}h2.chtitle{font-size:15pt;font-weight:bold;}h2{font-size:13.5pt;}h3{font-size:12.5pt;}p{text-align:justify;margin:0 0 12px;}.refs p{padding-left:36px;text-indent:-36px;text-align:left;}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;}th,td{border:1px solid #999;padding:6px 8px;vertical-align:top;font-size:11pt;}th{background:#f0f0f0;}.caption{font-style:italic;text-align:center;font-size:10.5pt;color:#555;}h1.chapter{page-break-before:always;}.pagebreak{page-break-before:always;}</style></head><body>

  

  <h1 class="chapter" align="center">Chapter Five</h1>
  <h2 class="chtitle" align="center">Summary, Conclusion and Recommendations</h2>

  <!-- 5.1 -->
  <h2>5.1 Introduction</h2>
  <p>
    This final chapter draws the study to a close. It begins by summarising what was done across the
    preceding chapters, then states the conclusions that can fairly be drawn from the work, sets out its
    specific contributions to knowledge and practice, acknowledges its limitations honestly, and offers
    recommendations for future development. The purpose is not to introduce new material but to step back and
    assess, in light of the aim and objectives stated in Chapter One, what the study has achieved and what it
    points towards next.
  </p>

  <!-- 5.2 -->
  <h2>5.2 Summary of the Study</h2>
  <p>
    The study set out to enhance and thoroughly document FanMidCommerce, a full-stack e-commerce web
    application, in order to demonstrate the practical use of modern web technologies and to assess the
    platform's suitability for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in the Nigerian context. The work
    unfolded across the chapters as follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Chapter One</b> established the problem. It showed that, although internet and mobile access in Nigeria
    have grown to the point where tens of millions of people transact online, the great majority of the
    country's roughly 39 million MSMEs have not moved onto a proper e-commerce platform, held back by cost,
    limited technical capacity, concerns about trust, and the poor contextual fit of generic international
    tools. It also identified a parallel gap on the technical side: modern web technologies such as Next.js,
    TypeScript, and GraphQL are powerful and widely used in industry, yet there is little academic
    documentation of how they fit together end to end in a complete online store, and even less that ties
    such work to the realities of the Nigerian market.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Chapter Two</b> reviewed the relevant literature. It clarified the core concepts, set the work within a
    theoretical framework combining Design Science Research with the Technology Acceptance Model and Diffusion
    of Innovations theory, examined the Nigerian e-commerce and SME context with concrete statistics, and
    surveyed both the empirical research on the chosen technologies and the existing systems on the market.
    The review confirmed a clear gap that the study was positioned to fill.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Chapter Three</b> set out the methodology and design. Following a design science approach realised
    through an iterative, agile-inspired process, it specified the system's requirements and presented its
    layered architecture, database design, GraphQL API, interface, and security design, including the
    resolution of the authentication-context challenge identified earlier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Chapter Four</b> reported the implementation and results. It documented the real folder structure,
    database schemas, and GraphQL API, walked through each module with code drawn from the actual codebase,
    and presented the results of functional testing across twenty-five test cases together with a performance
    evaluation of the deployed application. The evidence showed a working, deployed system that behaved as
    required.
  </p>

  <!-- 5.3 -->
  <h2>5.3 Achievement of Objectives</h2>
  <p>
    It is useful to measure the study directly against the six objectives stated in Chapter One. Table 5.1
    maps each objective to where and how it was met.
  </p>
  <table class="fig">
    <tr><th>#</th><th>Objective</th><th>How it was achieved</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Review relevant literature</td><td>Chapter Two: conceptual, theoretical, contextual, and empirical review with real sources.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Analyse the existing codebase</td><td>Chapters Three and Four: system analysis, architectural mapping, and identification of enhancements.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Implement additional features (search, orders, payments, reviews)</td><td>Chapter Four: server-side search and pagination, order management, Paystack payment, and verified reviews implemented and tested.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Resolve the GraphQL authentication-context challenge</td><td>Chapter Four (§4.5): per-request context built and verified on the server, with a complementary Edge route gate.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Evaluate performance, usability, and security</td><td>Chapter Four (§4.17–4.18): functional testing, Lighthouse performance audit, and an OWASP-informed security design.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Produce documentation and assess SME applicability</td><td>The dissertation itself plus deployment documentation; applicability discussed in §5.4 and §5.6.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="caption" align="center">Table 5.1: Mapping of objectives to their achievement.</p>
  <p>
    On this basis, all six objectives were met, and the overall aim, an enhanced, documented platform that
    demonstrates modern practice and is fitted to the SME context, was accomplished.
  </p>

  <!-- 5.4 -->
  <h2>5.4 Conclusion</h2>
  <p>
    Two broad conclusions follow from the study. The first is technical. It is both feasible and beneficial to
    build a complete, production-oriented e-commerce platform on a modern stack of Next.js, TypeScript,
    GraphQL, and MongoDB. The combination delivers real advantages, server rendering improves speed and
    discoverability, static typing improves reliability, and GraphQL provides precise, efficient data access,
    while the specific difficulty of propagating authentication context within the Next.js App Router can be
    resolved cleanly by verifying the token and assembling the context in the GraphQL route handler. The study
    therefore contributes a worked, documented answer to a question that the existing literature had left
    largely open.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second conclusion is practical and contextual. By separating a reusable commerce engine from a
    customisable presentation layer, and by supporting locally relevant payment channels, naira pricing, and
    owner-managed operation, the platform addresses the very barriers that the literature identified as
    holding Nigerian SMEs back from formal e-commerce. The result is not merely a prototype but a model that
    could realistically be deployed for a small business at low cost, and re-skinned for many such businesses
    from a single tested core. In short, the study shows that a modern, well-engineered platform and the needs
    of a developing-market SME are not in tension; with the right architectural choices, the one can be made
    to serve the other.
  </p>

  <!-- 5.5 -->
  <h2>5.5 Contributions to Knowledge</h2>
  <p>This study makes the following contributions:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A fully functional, enhanced, and documented e-commerce web application built on a modern full-stack
      JavaScript architecture, available as a reference implementation.</li>
    <li>A documented resolution of the authentication-context propagation problem within the Next.js App
      Router GraphQL endpoint, an issue inadequately addressed in the existing literature.</li>
    <li>An end-to-end account of the design and implementation of a full-stack e-commerce system using a
      TypeScript-first methodology, from database schema to user interface, suitable for teaching and for
      guiding practice.</li>
    <li>A reusable architectural model, separating a commerce engine from interchangeable presentation themes,
      that reduces the time and cost of delivering bespoke stores for multiple clients.</li>
    <li>A contextual analysis connecting modern architectural practice to the concrete requirements of SME
      e-commerce in the Nigerian digital economy.</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- 5.6 -->
  <h2>5.6 Limitations of the Study</h2>
  <p>
    The conclusions should be read in light of several limitations, stated honestly. First, the platform was
    evaluated in a development and staging context; it was not subjected to large-scale, real-world
    transactional load, so its behaviour under heavy concurrent traffic remains to be confirmed. Second,
    payment integration was exercised in sandbox mode in accordance with the regulatory requirements for live
    transactions, meaning that the live-payment configuration is documented but not transacted against. Third,
    usability evidence rests on heuristic evaluation and a small number of representative users rather than a
    large-sample study, which limits statistical generalisation. Fourth, the findings are framed within the
    Nigerian context and may require adaptation before applying elsewhere. None of these limitations undermines
    the study's central contributions, but each marks a sensible boundary on the strength of its claims.
  </p>

  <!-- 5.7 -->
  <h2>5.7 Recommendations</h2>
  <h3>5.7.1 Recommendations for practice</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Small businesses seeking an online presence should consider a reusable, self-owned platform of this
      kind as a lower-cost alternative to recurring subscription services, particularly where local payment
      channels and naira pricing matter.</li>
    <li>Developers building on a Next.js and GraphQL stack should verify authentication tokens and assemble the
      request context at the GraphQL route handler, and enforce authorisation in resolvers, rather than relying
      on the client interface to hide privileged actions.</li>
    <li>For any store handling payment, prices should be recomputed on the server and payment outcomes verified
      with the provider, with order finalisation made idempotent to prevent duplicate orders.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>5.7.2 Recommendations for future work</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Load and scalability testing:</b> evaluate the platform under realistic concurrent load and tune
      database indexing and caching accordingly.</li>
    <li><b>Live deployment study:</b> deploy with a real SME and measure actual sales, conversion, and user
      satisfaction over time, moving beyond the staging evaluation conducted here.</li>
    <li><b>Expanded payment and logistics options:</b> integrate additional gateways and delivery/logistics
      providers relevant to the Nigerian market.</li>
    <li><b>Analytics and recommendations:</b> add product recommendation and richer analytics to support
      data-driven merchandising.</li>
    <li><b>Theme marketplace:</b> extend the theming system into a library of ready-made themes, further
      reducing the effort of launching a new client store.</li>
    <li><b>Formal usability study:</b> conduct a larger usability evaluation using a validated instrument such
      as the System Usability Scale to strengthen the evidence base.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- 5.8 -->
  <h2>5.8 Concluding Remark</h2>
  <p>
    This study began from a simple observation: that a great many capable Nigerian businesses remain offline,
    not for lack of customers or ambition, but for lack of an affordable, trustworthy, and locally suitable
    way to sell online. By designing, building, documenting, and evaluating FanMidCommerce, the study has
    shown that modern web technology can be marshalled to meet that need, and has left behind a working system
    and a reusable model that others can learn from and build upon. The work is offered both as a completed
    piece of research and as a foundation for the continued, practical digital inclusion of small enterprises.
  </p>

